/* 生成的文件，不要手改：node tools/phonon-build.mjs 从 tools/phonon/ 生成（inputs 1611af55168c2e1c，公式由 katex@0.16.11 转成 MathML）。 */
window.MOLASTRIA_PHONON = {"index":{"title":"Phonons","sub":"The quanta of lattice vibration: from a chain of atoms to thermal conductivity and machine-learned potentials","eyebrow":"MOLASTRIA · QUASIPARTICLES · PHONONS","h1":"Phonons","question":"Why does diamond feel cold to the touch, yet absorb almost no heat at low temperature?","lead":"Atoms in a crystal are not at rest: tied to their lattice sites by chemical bonds, they vibrate together. Break the vibration into normal modes, each with a definite wavevector and frequency, then count each mode’s energy in quantum-mechanical portions: one portion is a <b>phonon</b>. Phonons set a solid’s heat capacity, thermal expansion and thermal conductivity, govern how neutrons and light scatter in a crystal, and bind electrons together into superconductivity. They are the most typical quasiparticle, and the easiest to work out completely. This hall has nine chapters: the monatomic chain, the diatomic chain, the Brillouin zone and dispersion in three dimensions, phonons as quanta and the density of states, heat capacity, how phonons are measured, anharmonicity and thermal expansion, lattice thermal conductivity, and how phonons are computed, with machine-learned potentials and the frontier. All eleven demos are computed live in the browser.","scope":"<b>How to read the labels.</b> Every chapter and every key point carries a status: history, theorem (derived rigorously from mechanics, quantum mechanics or statistical mechanics), established by experiment, approximation (valid under the stated conditions), frontier and open. Demos carry one of three labels: exact (computed live from the model’s definition, with no further approximation), toy model (built to show one thing clearly) and data (numbers from textbooks or papers). Numbers for specific materials are common literature values, with sources given; the maths of the demos is written from textbook formulas, with no code copied from programs such as phonopy or ShengBTE. This hall is part of the <a href=\"../../quasiparticles/\">Quasiparticles</a> section.","levels":{"history":["History","Documented history and facts about the literature"],"proved":["Theorem","Derived rigorously from mechanics, quantum mechanics or statistical mechanics"],"tested":["Established","Supported by several independent experiments or calculations, and in everyday use"],"approx":["Approximation","Holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them"],"frontier":["Frontier","Developing fast; conclusions and best numbers are still changing"],"open":["Open","Not yet achieved or explained by anyone"]},"demoKinds":{"exact":["Exact","Computed live from the model’s definition (the model is described under each demo)"],"toy":["Toy model","A minimal model built to show one thing clearly; its parameters do not correspond to a real material"],"data":["Data","Numbers from textbooks or papers; the demo only computes and plots"]},"groups":[{"title":"Intuition in one dimension","ids":["chain","diatomic"]},{"title":"Crystals and quanta","ids":["bz","quantum","heat"]},{"title":"Seeing phonons","ids":["measure"]},{"title":"Anharmonicity and transport","ids":["anharm","transport"]},{"title":"Computation and the frontier","ids":["compute"]}],"chapters":[{"id":"chain","n":"01","title":"The one-dimensional chain: normal modes and dispersion","summary":"Replace the chemical bonds between atoms by springs, keep only terms quadratic in the displacements (the harmonic approximation), and Newton’s equations become linear, solved by plane waves of definite wavevector q. How the frequency depends on q, ω(q), is the dispersion relation: sound speed times q for long waves, bending over at short waves, flat at the zone boundary. Periodic boundary conditions give N atoms exactly N modes.","level":"proved","demos":["chain"]},{"id":"diatomic","n":"02","title":"The diatomic chain: acoustic and optical branches and the gap","summary":"Put two atoms in each cell and the dispersion splits into two branches. The acoustic branch is sound at long wavelength, both kinds of atom moving together; on the optical branch at q = 0 the two move against each other, and in ionic crystals this couples strongly to infrared light. Between the branches lies a range of frequencies at which no vibration can propagate. With p atoms per cell there are p branches in one dimension and 3p in three.","level":"proved","demos":["diatomic"]},{"id":"bz","n":"03","title":"Three-dimensional crystals: the reciprocal lattice, the Brillouin zone and band plots","summary":"In two and three dimensions the wavevector q becomes a vector and the Brillouin zone a polygon or polyhedron in reciprocal space. The dispersion is no longer a curve but 3p surfaces, which are cut open along paths joining high-symmetry points (Γ, X, M, K, L…) for plotting. Every mode has a polarisation: longitudinal waves vibrate along q, transverse ones perpendicular to it.","level":"proved","demos":["bz"]},{"id":"quantum","n":"04","title":"Quantisation: the phonon as a quasiparticle","summary":"Each normal mode is a harmonic oscillator, whose energy in quantum mechanics can only be ħω(n + ½). Read n as “this mode holds n phonons” and the lattice vibrations become a gas of bosons whose number is not conserved: temperature sets the mean number in each mode, and zero-point energy keeps the crystal jiggling even at absolute zero. Counting all modes by frequency gives the density of states, which fixes almost every thermodynamic property. Phonons also trade energy with electrons, which is where conventional superconductivity comes from.","level":"proved","demos":["dos"]},{"id":"heat","n":"05","title":"Heat capacity: Dulong–Petit, Einstein and Debye","summary":"Classical equipartition gives 3k_B of heat capacity per atom, true for many elements near room temperature and entirely wrong at low temperature. Einstein let every atom vibrate at one frequency and quantised it, explaining the fall; Debye treated the crystal as an elastic continuum cut off at a maximum frequency and got the T³ law that matches experiment. Real densities of states lie between the two, and the Debye temperature is a handy yardstick.","level":"tested","demos":["cv"]},{"id":"measure","n":"06","title":"Seeing phonons: neutrons, X-rays, Raman and infrared","summary":"To measure a phonon a probe must hand the lattice both energy ħω and crystal momentum ħq. Thermal neutrons have energies and wavelengths of just the same order as phonons, so inelastic neutron scattering can map a whole dispersion point by point; inelastic X-ray scattering does the same with synchrotron light. Visible light’s wavevector is too small, so Raman and infrared see only the zone centre, but with high precision and cheap instruments, and the ratio of Stokes to anti-Stokes peaks even works as a thermometer.","level":"tested","demos":["scatter","raman"]},{"id":"anharm","n":"07","title":"Anharmonicity: thermal expansion, lifetimes, soft modes and FPU","summary":"In the harmonic approximation phonons never collide, crystals do not expand on heating and thermal conductivity is infinite. Real potentials have cubic and quartic terms: they make frequencies depend on volume (the quasi-harmonic approximation and the Grüneisen parameter), make phonons scatter one another with finite lifetimes, and make some modes soften with temperature and drive phase transitions. The FPU computer experiment of 1955 set out to watch an anharmonic chain thermalise and instead saw the energy come almost all the way back, opening up solitons and nonlinear dynamics.","level":"approx","demos":["qha","fpu"]},{"id":"transport","n":"08","title":"Heat conduction: the phonon gas, umklapp and first-principles conductivity","summary":"In insulators heat is carried by phonons. Treat them as a gas and the conductivity is κ = ⅓ C v ℓ. The mean free path ℓ is limited by boundaries, impurities and phonon–phonon collisions; but only umklapp processes, which do not conserve crystal momentum, produce thermal resistance, Peierls’ insight of 1929. So conductivity first rises and then falls with temperature, with a peak in between. Today solving the phonon Boltzmann equation predicts conductivity from first principles: boron arsenide was computed first and measured afterwards.","level":"tested","demos":["kappa"]},{"id":"compute","n":"09","title":"Computing phonons: frozen phonons, perturbation theory and machine-learned potentials","summary":"The core of a phonon calculation is the force constants: move one atom a little and see how hard the others are pushed. Two routes: actually displace atoms in a supercell, compute forces with density functional theory and take differences (the finite-displacement method, as in phonopy), or compute the response at any q directly with density-functional perturbation theory. Both need care with supercell size, displacement size, force accuracy and whether imaginary frequencies are real. In recent years equivariant neural-network potentials and universal “foundation models” have made phonon calculations several orders of magnitude faster, and their accuracy is being tested systematically.","level":"tested","demos":["frozen"]}],"milestones":[{"year":"1819","text":"Dulong and Petit: many solid elements have nearly the same molar heat capacity, about 25 J/(mol·K) (<a href=\"#c-heat\" data-open=\"heat\">05 · Heat capacity: Dulong–Petit, Einstein and Debye</a>)."},{"year":"1907","text":"Einstein applies Planck’s quanta to vibrations in solids and explains the fall of heat capacity at low temperature <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1912","text":"Debye’s continuum model and T³ law <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-debye1912\" data-ref=\"debye1912\">Debye 1912</a>]</span>; Born and von Kármán set out lattice dynamics with periodic boundary conditions (<a href=\"#c-chain\" data-open=\"chain\">01 · The one-dimensional chain: normal modes and dispersion</a>); Grüneisen’s equation of state <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-grueneisen1912\" data-ref=\"grueneisen1912\">Grüneisen 1912</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1928","text":"Raman and Krishnan discover inelastic scattering of light by molecules and crystals <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-raman1928\" data-ref=\"raman1928\">Raman &amp; Krishnan 1928</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1929","text":"Peierls introduces umklapp processes to explain the thermal resistance of insulators (<a href=\"#c-transport\" data-open=\"transport\">08 · Heat conduction: the phonon gas, umklapp and first-principles conductivity</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-peierls1929\" data-ref=\"peierls1929\">Peierls 1929</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1950","text":"The isotope effect in mercury: the superconducting transition temperature changes with isotope mass, pointing to electron–phonon interaction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-maxwell1950\" data-ref=\"maxwell1950\">Maxwell 1950</a>; <a href=\"#ref-reynolds1950\" data-ref=\"reynolds1950\">Reynolds et al. 1950</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1955","text":"Brockhouse measures phonons in aluminium with neutrons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1955\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1955\">Brockhouse &amp; Stewart 1955</a>]</span>; the computer experiment of Fermi, Pasta, Ulam and Tsingou is reported (<a href=\"#c-anharm\" data-open=\"anharm\">07 · Anharmonicity: thermal expansion, lifetimes, soft modes and FPU</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauxois2008\" data-ref=\"dauxois2008\">Dauxois 2008</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1957","text":"BCS theory: phonon exchange pairs electrons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bcs1957\" data-ref=\"bcs1957\">Bardeen, Cooper &amp; Schrieffer 1957</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1987","text":"Density-functional perturbation theory for phonons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-baroni1987\" data-ref=\"baroni1987\">Baroni, Giannozzi &amp; Testa 1987</a>]</span>; the first phonon dispersion measured by inelastic X-ray scattering <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dorner1987\" data-ref=\"dorner1987\">Dorner et al. 1987</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1994","text":"Brockhouse receives the Nobel Prize in Physics for neutron spectroscopy (shared with Shull) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1995\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1995\">Brockhouse 1995</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1997","text":"The supercell finite-displacement (“direct”) method <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-parlinski1997\" data-ref=\"parlinski1997\">Parlinski, Li &amp; Kawazoe 1997</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2007","text":"First-principles lattice thermal conductivity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-broido2007\" data-ref=\"broido2007\">Broido et al. 2007</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2018","text":"High thermal conductivity in boron arsenide measured by three groups at once <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kang2018\" data-ref=\"kang2018\">Kang et al. 2018</a>; <a href=\"#ref-li2018\" data-ref=\"li2018\">Li et al. 2018</a>; <a href=\"#ref-tian2018\" data-ref=\"tian2018\">Tian et al. 2018</a>]</span>; chiral phonons observed <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zhu2018\" data-ref=\"zhu2018\">Zhu et al. 2018</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2019","text":"Second sound in graphite above 100 K <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huberman2019\" data-ref=\"huberman2019\">Huberman et al. 2019</a>]</span>; a unified theory of thermal transport in crystals and glasses <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-simoncelli2019\" data-ref=\"simoncelli2019\">Simoncelli, Marzari &amp; Mauri 2019</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025","text":"A large-scale test of universal machine-learned potentials on phonons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-loew2025\" data-ref=\"loew2025\">Loew et al. 2025</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-compute\" data-open=\"compute\">09 · Computing phonons: frozen phonons, perturbation theory and machine-learned potentials</a>)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"For a monatomic chain, how are q and q + 2π/a related?","opts":["Two different modes","The same vibration: the atomic displacements are identical","Same frequency, opposite displacements","The latter is not allowed"],"a":1,"why":"Atoms sit only on lattice sites, and e^{i(q+2π/a)ja} = e^{iqja}. So only the first Brillouin zone is needed.","ch":"chain"},{"q":"In the diatomic chain, what sets the acoustic-branch frequency at the zone boundary?","opts":["The lighter atom","The heavier atom: √(2K/M)","The reduced mass of the two","The total mass"],"a":1,"why":"At the boundary the light atoms stand still and only the heavy ones move, ω = √(2K/M); the optical branch is √(2K/m).","ch":"diatomic"},{"q":"A crystal with 5 atoms per primitive cell (perovskite SrTiO₃, say) has how many phonon branches, and how many of them acoustic?","opts":["5, with 1 acoustic","15, with 3 acoustic","15, with 5 acoustic","10, with 2 acoustic"],"a":1,"why":"3 × atoms = 15 branches; in three dimensions there are always 3 acoustic branches (the whole cell translating in three directions), and the other 12 are optical.","ch":"bz"},{"q":"At 300 K, what is the mean occupation of a phonon mode with ħω = k_BT?","opts":["1","0.58","0.5","e⁻¹ ≈ 0.37"],"a":1,"why":"Bose–Einstein n = 1/(eˣ − 1); at x = 1, n = 1/(e − 1) ≈ 0.582. Classical equipartition holds only for x ≪ 1.","ch":"quantum"},{"q":"At low temperature, the Debye model gives a lattice heat capacity proportional to what?","opts":["T","T²","T³","e^{−Θ/T}"],"a":2,"why":"Only long-wavelength acoustic phonons are excited at low temperature; with a density of states ∝ ω² the integral gives T³. The Einstein model drops exponentially, faster than experiment.","ch":"heat"},{"q":"Why does first-order Raman scattering see only phonons at the zone centre?","opts":["Because optical phonons exist only at Γ","Because visible light’s wavevector is two to three orders of magnitude smaller than the zone, and momentum is conserved","Because of selection rules","Because Raman scattering does not conserve energy"],"a":1,"why":"Light can hand the lattice at most 2k_light, about one per cent of the zone. Selection rules then decide which Γ-point modes are Raman active.","ch":"measure"},{"q":"In inelastic neutron scattering, why does the “neutron gains energy” peak almost vanish at low temperature?","opts":["The neutrons slow down","To absorb a phonon one must already be thermally excited; the intensity ∝ n(ω, T)","Momentum is not conserved","Detectors stop working at low temperature"],"a":1,"why":"Creation ∝ n + 1 and absorption ∝ n, with ratio e^{−ħω/k_BT} (detailed balance).","ch":"measure"},{"q":"Does a strictly harmonic crystal expand on heating?","opts":["Yes, the amplitude grows","No: a harmonic oscillator’s mean position does not depend on amplitude","Only at high temperature","It depends on the crystal structure"],"a":1,"why":"Thermal expansion needs anharmonicity; the quasi-harmonic approximation includes it through volume-dependent frequencies, with a strength given by the Grüneisen parameter γ.","ch":"anharm"},{"q":"In the FPU computer experiment, what happened after the energy was put into the lowest mode?","opts":["It spread quickly over all modes","It sloshed among the first few modes and after a while came almost entirely back to the first","The chain broke","It stayed in the first mode and never left"],"a":1,"why":"That is the FPU recurrence. It was later connected to solitons and the KAM theorem; with enough energy and time the chain does eventually thermalise.","ch":"anharm"},{"q":"Can “normal” three-phonon processes, which conserve crystal momentum, alone produce thermal resistance?","opts":["Yes, any collision does","No: the total phonon momentum is unchanged so heat flow does not decay; umklapp (or boundaries, defects) is needed","Only at low temperature","Only in metals"],"a":1,"why":"That is Peierls’ argument of 1929. When normal processes are frequent and umklapp rare, phonons flow like a fluid and second sound can appear.","ch":"transport"},{"q":"In a finite-displacement phonon calculation, which q points are exact in the supercell?","opts":["Every q","Only those commensurate with the supercell (N of them for N cells)","Only Γ","Only the zone boundary"],"a":1,"why":"The supercell’s periodicity makes the force-constant sums exact at q = 2πn/(Na); everything else is Fourier interpolation, which fails when interactions reach beyond half the supercell.","ch":"compute"},{"q":"What does an imaginary frequency (ω² &lt; 0) in a calculation usually mean?","opts":["The calculation must be wrong","The structure is unstable along that mode (or the calculation settings are at fault, which must be told apart)","The mode has a very high frequency","The mode cannot be measured"],"a":1,"why":"The energy curves downward along that direction: the structure will distort to lower symmetry (soft mode, phase transition). But too small a supercell, inaccurate forces or poor relaxation can also produce spurious imaginary frequencies.","ch":"compute"}],"books":[{"title":"Introduction to Solid State Physics","authors":"Charles Kittel (Wiley, 8th ed. 2005)","note":"Chapters 4 and 5 are the standard introduction to phonons and thermal properties; the table of Debye temperatures in chapter 5 is used directly here."},{"title":"Solid State Physics","authors":"Neil W. Ashcroft, N. David Mermin (Saunders, 1976)","note":"Chapters 22–27: classical and quantum theory of the harmonic crystal, neutron scattering and anharmonic effects, with complete derivations."},{"title":"Dynamical Theory of Crystal Lattices","authors":"Max Born, Kun Huang (Oxford, 1954)","note":"The classic of lattice dynamics by Born and Huang Kun, the first systematic account of force constants, dynamical matrices and the long-wavelength limit."},{"title":"Introduction to Lattice Dynamics","authors":"Martin T. Dove (Cambridge, 1993)","note":"Short and clear, from the one-dimensional chain to neutron scattering and soft-mode transitions; a good companion to this hall."},{"title":"Electrons and Phonons","authors":"J. M. Ziman (Oxford, 1960)","note":"The classic of transport theory: the phonon Boltzmann equation, normal and umklapp processes, electron–phonon scattering."},{"title":"Introduction to the Theory of Thermal Neutron Scattering","authors":"G. L. Squires (Cambridge, 1978; reissued 2012)","note":"Derivations of neutron scattering cross-sections; the one-phonon formula of chapter 6 comes from here."},{"title":"Fundamentals of Semiconductors","authors":"Peter Y. Yu, Manuel Cardona (Springer, 4th ed. 2010)","note":"Phonons in semiconductors, infrared and Raman spectroscopy, LO–TO splitting, with abundant measured data."}],"open":[{"q":"In strongly anharmonic materials (near phase transitions, ionic conductors, thermoelectrics), to what extent is the “phonon” still a good quasiparticle?","ch":"anharm"},{"q":"Can phonon hydrodynamics and second sound be used to engineer heat flow in materials at ordinary temperatures?","ch":"transport"},{"q":"The thermal conductivity of glasses and disordered materials: how should vibrations between “phonons” and “diffusons” be described in one framework?","ch":"transport"},{"q":"Can universal machine-learned potentials reliably predict anharmonic properties (thermal conductivity, high-temperature phase stability), not just harmonic frequencies?","ch":"compute"},{"q":"Can chiral phonons carry observable angular momentum and magnetic moment, and be used to control spins?","ch":"quantum"}],"notes":{"map":"Each card is a chapter. The label gives the status of the bulk of the chapter; key points inside carry their own labels. Chapters you have opened are ticked (on this device only).","history":"Years are publication years (preprints by the year of the preprint).","path":"To read on: the <a href=\"../../quasiparticles/\">Quasiparticles</a> section sets phonons among their siblings, magnons, excitons, polarons, plasmons and Cooper pairs; the <a href=\"../../chemistry/structural/#c-crystals\">crystals</a> and <a href=\"../../chemistry/structural/#c-diffraction\">diffraction</a> chapters of the Structural Chemistry hall cover the lattice phonons live on; the Spectra Hall’s <a href=\"../../spectra/#m-raman\">Raman</a>, <a href=\"../../spectra/#m-ir\">infrared</a> and <a href=\"../../spectra/#m-ins\">inelastic neutron scattering</a> pages show how vibrations are seen in the lab; <a href=\"../../macro/#c-statmech\">Heat, Statistics and Flow</a> covers the statistical mechanics behind heat capacity and entropy; the <a href=\"../../optics/#c-xray\">X-ray diffraction</a> chapter of Optics covers the half of the story before the Debye–Waller factor; and <a href=\"../../nequip/#c-forces\">NequIP</a> explains how equivariant machine-learned potentials give accurate forces, which is exactly what phonon calculations need.","library":"53 references. Those with a DOI were looked up one by one in OpenAlex (which draws on Crossref) for title, authors, year and volume; those with only an arXiv number were checked on arXiv (November 2026; node tools/phonon-refs.mjs re-checks them)."}},"refs":[{"id":"einstein1907","authors":"A. Einstein","year":1907,"title":"Die Plancksche Theorie der Strahlung und die Theorie der spezifischen Wärme","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"327","pages":"180–190","doi":"10.1002/andp.19063270110","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum","heat"]},{"id":"debye1912","authors":"P. Debye","year":1912,"title":"Zur Theorie der spezifischen Wärmen","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"344","pages":"789–839","doi":"10.1002/andp.19123441404","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["chain","heat"]},{"id":"grueneisen1912","authors":"E. Grüneisen","year":1912,"title":"Theorie des festen Zustandes einatomiger Elemente","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"344","pages":"257–306","doi":"10.1002/andp.19123441202","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"raman1928","authors":"C. V. Raman, K. S. Krishnan","year":1928,"title":"A new type of secondary radiation","journal":"Nature","vol":"121","pages":"501–502","doi":"10.1038/121501c0","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"peierls1929","authors":"R. Peierls","year":1929,"title":"Zur kinetischen Theorie der Wärmeleitung in Kristallen","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"395","pages":"1055–1101","doi":"10.1002/andp.19293950803","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"lyddane1941","authors":"R. H. Lyddane, R. G. Sachs, E. Teller","year":1941,"title":"On the polar vibrations of alkali halides","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"59","pages":"673–676","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.59.673","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["diatomic","measure"]},{"id":"maxwell1950","authors":"E. Maxwell","year":1950,"title":"Isotope effect in the superconductivity of mercury","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"78","pages":"477","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.78.477","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum"]},{"id":"reynolds1950","authors":"C. A. Reynolds, B. Serin, W. H. Wright, L. B. Nesbitt","year":1950,"title":"Superconductivity of isotopes of mercury","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"78","pages":"487","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.78.487","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum"]},{"id":"brockhouse1955","authors":"B. N. Brockhouse, A. T. Stewart","year":1955,"title":"Scattering of neutrons by phonons in an aluminum single crystal","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"100","pages":"756–757","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.100.756","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"bcs1957","authors":"J. Bardeen, L. N. Cooper, J. R. Schrieffer","year":1957,"title":"Theory of superconductivity","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"108","pages":"1175–1204","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.108.1175","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum"]},{"id":"callaway1959","authors":"J. Callaway","year":1959,"title":"Model for lattice thermal conductivity at low temperatures","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"113","pages":"1046–1051","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.113.1046","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"cochran1960","authors":"W. Cochran","year":1960,"title":"Crystal stability and the theory of ferroelectricity","journal":"Advances in Physics","vol":"9","pages":"387–423","doi":"10.1080/00018736000101229","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"zabusky1965","authors":"N. J. Zabusky, M. D. Kruskal","year":1965,"title":"Interaction of \"solitons\" in a collisionless plasma and the recurrence of initial states","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"15","pages":"240–243","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.15.240","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"stedman1966","authors":"R. Stedman, G. Nilsson","year":1966,"title":"Dispersion relations for phonons in aluminum at 80 and 300°K","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"145","pages":"492–500","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.145.492","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bz","measure"]},{"id":"svensson1967","authors":"E. C. Svensson, B. N. Brockhouse, J. M. Rowe","year":1967,"title":"Crystal dynamics of copper","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"155","pages":"619–632","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.155.619","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["chain","bz","measure"]},{"id":"hart1970","authors":"T. R. Hart, R. L. Aggarwal, B. Lax","year":1970,"title":"Temperature dependence of Raman scattering in silicon","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"1","pages":"638–642","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.1.638","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"baroni1987","authors":"S. Baroni, P. Giannozzi, A. Testa","year":1987,"title":"Green’s-function approach to linear response in solids","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"58","pages":"1861–1864","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.58.1861","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"dorner1987","authors":"B. Dorner, E. Burkel, Th. Illini, J. Peisl","year":1987,"title":"First measurement of a phonon dispersion curve by inelastic X-ray scattering","journal":"Zeitschrift für Physik B","vol":"69","pages":"179–183","doi":"10.1007/BF01307274","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"giannozzi1991","authors":"P. Giannozzi, S. de Gironcoli, P. Pavone, S. Baroni","year":1991,"title":"Ab initio calculation of phonon dispersions in semiconductors","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"43","pages":"7231–7242","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.43.7231","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["diatomic","compute"]},{"id":"brockhouse1995","authors":"B. N. Brockhouse","year":1995,"title":"Slow neutron spectroscopy and the grand atlas of the physical world","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"67","pages":"735–751","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.67.735","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"mary1996","authors":"T. A. Mary, J. S. O. Evans, T. Vogt, A. W. Sleight","year":1996,"title":"Negative thermal expansion from 0.3 to 1050 Kelvin in ZrW2O8","journal":"Science","vol":"272","pages":"90–92","doi":"10.1126/science.272.5258.90","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"gonze1997","authors":"X. Gonze, C. Lee","year":1997,"title":"Dynamical matrices, Born effective charges, dielectric permittivity tensors, and interatomic force constants from density-functional perturbation theory","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"55","pages":"10355–10368","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.55.10355","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure","compute"]},{"id":"parlinski1997","authors":"K. Parlinski, Z. Q. Li, Y. Kawazoe","year":1997,"title":"First-principles determination of the soft mode in cubic ZrO2","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"78","pages":"4063–4066","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.78.4063","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"burkel2000","authors":"E. Burkel","year":2000,"title":"Phonon spectroscopy by inelastic x-ray scattering","journal":"Reports on Progress in Physics","vol":"63","pages":"171–232","doi":"10.1088/0034-4885/63/2/203","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"baroni2001","authors":"S. Baroni, S. de Gironcoli, A. Dal Corso, P. Giannozzi","year":2001,"title":"Phonons and related crystal properties from density-functional perturbation theory","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"73","pages":"515–562","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.73.515","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"berman2005","authors":"G. P. Berman, F. M. Izrailev","year":2005,"title":"The Fermi–Pasta–Ulam problem: fifty years of progress","journal":"Chaos","vol":"15","pages":"015104","doi":"10.1063/1.1855036","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"mounet2005","authors":"N. Mounet, N. Marzari","year":2005,"title":"First-principles determination of the structural, vibrational and thermodynamic properties of diamond, graphite, and derivatives","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"71","pages":"205214","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.71.205214","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bz","heat"]},{"id":"broido2007","authors":"D. A. Broido, M. Malorny, G. Birner, N. Mingo, D. A. Stewart","year":2007,"title":"Intrinsic lattice thermal conductivity of semiconductors from first principles","journal":"Applied Physics Letters","vol":"91","pages":"231922","doi":"10.1063/1.2822891","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"balandin2008","authors":"A. A. Balandin, S. Ghosh, W. Bao, I. Calizo, D. Teweldebrhan, F. Miao, C. N. Lau","year":2008,"title":"Superior thermal conductivity of single-layer graphene","journal":"Nano Letters","vol":"8","pages":"902–907","doi":"10.1021/nl0731872","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"dauxois2008","authors":"T. Dauxois","year":2008,"title":"Fermi, Pasta, Ulam, and a mysterious lady","journal":"Physics Today","vol":"61","pages":"55–57","doi":"10.1063/1.2835154","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"fultz2010","authors":"B. Fultz","year":2010,"title":"Vibrational thermodynamics of materials","journal":"Progress in Materials Science","vol":"55","pages":"247–352","doi":"10.1016/j.pmatsci.2009.05.002","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum","heat"]},{"id":"hellman2011","authors":"O. Hellman, I. A. Abrikosov, S. I. Simak","year":2011,"title":"Lattice dynamics of anharmonic solids from first principles","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"84","pages":"180301","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.84.180301","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"errea2013","authors":"I. Errea, M. Calandra, F. Mauri","year":2013,"title":"First-principles theory of anharmonicity and the inverse isotope effect in superconducting palladium-hydride compounds","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"111","pages":"177002","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.111.177002","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm"]},{"id":"lindsay2013","authors":"L. Lindsay, D. A. Broido, T. L. Reinecke","year":2013,"title":"First-principles determination of ultrahigh thermal conductivity of boron arsenide: a competitor for diamond?","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"111","pages":"025901","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.111.025901","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"li2014","authors":"W. Li, J. Carrete, N. A. Katcho, N. Mingo","year":2014,"title":"ShengBTE: a solver of the Boltzmann transport equation for phonons","journal":"Computer Physics Communications","vol":"185","pages":"1747–1758","doi":"10.1016/j.cpc.2014.02.015","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"drozdov2015","authors":"A. P. Drozdov, M. I. Eremets, I. A. Troyan, V. Ksenofontov, S. I. Shylin","year":2015,"title":"Conventional superconductivity at 203 kelvin at high pressures in the sulfur hydride system","journal":"Nature","vol":"525","pages":"73–76","doi":"10.1038/nature14964","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum"]},{"id":"togo2015","authors":"A. Togo, I. Tanaka","year":2015,"title":"First principles phonon calculations in materials science","journal":"Scripta Materialia","vol":"108","pages":"1–5","doi":"10.1016/j.scriptamat.2015.07.021","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"togo2015b","authors":"A. Togo, L. Chaput, I. Tanaka","year":2015,"title":"Distributions of phonon lifetimes in Brillouin zones","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"91","pages":"094306","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.91.094306","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anharm","transport"]},{"id":"chu2017","authors":"Y. Chu, P. Kharel, W. H. Renninger, L. D. Burkhart, L. Frunzio, P. T. Rakich, R. J. Schoelkopf","year":2017,"title":"Quantum acoustics with superconducting qubits","journal":"Science","vol":"358","pages":"199–202","doi":"10.1126/science.aao1511","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum"]},{"id":"giustino2017","authors":"F. Giustino","year":2017,"title":"Electron-phonon interactions from first principles","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"89","pages":"015003","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.89.015003","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum"]},{"id":"kang2018","authors":"J. S. Kang, M. Li, H. Wu, H. Nguyen, Y. Hu","year":2018,"title":"Experimental observation of high thermal conductivity in boron arsenide","journal":"Science","vol":"361","pages":"575–578","doi":"10.1126/science.aat5522","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"li2018","authors":"S. Li, Q. Zheng, Y. Lv, X. Liu, X. Wang, P. Y. Huang, D. G. Cahill, B. Lv","year":2018,"title":"High thermal conductivity in cubic boron arsenide crystals","journal":"Science","vol":"361","pages":"579–581","doi":"10.1126/science.aat8982","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"lindsay2018","authors":"L. Lindsay, C. Hua, X. L. Ruan, S. Lee","year":2018,"title":"Survey of ab initio phonon thermal transport","journal":"Materials Today Physics","vol":"7","pages":"106–120","doi":"10.1016/j.mtphys.2018.11.008","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"tian2018","authors":"F. Tian, B. Song, X. Chen, N. K. Ravichandran, Y. Lv, K. Chen, S. Sullivan, J. Kim et al.","year":2018,"title":"Unusual high thermal conductivity in boron arsenide bulk crystals","journal":"Science","vol":"361","pages":"582–585","doi":"10.1126/science.aat7932","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"zhu2018","authors":"H. Zhu, J. Yi, M.-Y. Li, J. Xiao, L. Zhang, C.-W. Yang, R. A. Kaindl, L.-J. Li et al.","year":2018,"title":"Observation of chiral phonons","journal":"Science","vol":"359","pages":"579–582","doi":"10.1126/science.aar2711","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["quantum"]},{"id":"huberman2019","authors":"S. Huberman, R. A. Duncan, K. Chen, B. Song, V. Chiloyan, Z. Ding, A. A. Maznev, G. Chen et al.","year":2019,"title":"Observation of second sound in graphite at temperatures above 100 K","journal":"Science","vol":"364","pages":"375–379","doi":"10.1126/science.aav3548","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"simoncelli2019","authors":"M. Simoncelli, N. Marzari, F. Mauri","year":2019,"title":"Unified theory of thermal transport in crystals and glasses","journal":"Nature Physics","vol":"15","pages":"809–813","doi":"10.1038/s41567-019-0520-x","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["transport"]},{"id":"batatia2022","authors":"I. Batatia, D. P. Kovács, G. N. C. Simm, C. Ortner, G. Csányi","year":2022,"title":"MACE: higher order equivariant message passing neural networks for fast and accurate force fields","journal":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 35 (NeurIPS 2022)","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2206.07697","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"batzner2022","authors":"S. Batzner, A. Musaelian, L. Sun, M. Geiger, J. P. Mailoa, M. Kornbluth, N. Molinari, T. E. Smidt, B. Kozinsky","year":2022,"title":"E(3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials","journal":"Nature Communications","vol":"13","pages":"2453","doi":"10.1038/s41467-022-29939-5","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"deng2023","authors":"B. Deng, P. Zhong, K. Jun, J. Riebesell, K. Han, C. J. Bartel, G. Ceder","year":2023,"title":"CHGNet as a pretrained universal neural network potential for charge-informed atomistic modelling","journal":"Nature Machine Intelligence","vol":"5","pages":"1031–1041","doi":"10.1038/s42256-023-00716-3","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"togo2023","authors":"A. Togo","year":2023,"title":"First-principles phonon calculations with phonopy and phono3py","journal":"Journal of the Physical Society of Japan","vol":"92","pages":"012001","doi":"10.7566/JPSJ.92.012001","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]},{"id":"pota2024","authors":"B. Póta, P. Ahlawat, G. Csányi, M. Simoncelli","year":2024,"title":"Thermal conductivity predictions with foundation atomistic models","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2408.00755","url":"","note":"","used":["transport","compute"]},{"id":"loew2025","authors":"A. Loew, D. Sun, H.-C. Wang, S. Botti, M. A. L. Marques","year":2025,"title":"Universal machine learning interatomic potentials are ready for phonons","journal":"npj Computational Materials","vol":"11","pages":"","doi":"10.1038/s41524-025-01650-1","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["compute"]}],"chapters":{"chain":{"id":"chain","body":[{"p":"A crystal has 10²³ atoms, each jiggling about its lattice site and all coupled together: push one atom and its neighbours follow. It looks like a hopeless many-body problem, yet as long as the amplitudes are small it is one of the easiest problems in physics to solve exactly. The trick is a change of coordinates: instead of following each atom, follow the crystal’s “collective ways of vibrating”. Each way vibrates independently, like a set of oscillators that ignore one another."},{"h":"The harmonic approximation"},{"p":"Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> be the displacement of atom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> from its site <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Expand the total potential energy in all the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: the zeroth-order term is a constant, the first-order term vanishes at equilibrium (the net force on every atom is zero), and the lowest non-trivial term is quadratic. Keeping only that is the <b>harmonic approximation</b>. With nearest-neighbour springs the quadratic term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>K</mi><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, and Newton’s equation is"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>u</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"The equation is linear in all the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and every atom sees the same surroundings (translation symmetry). Solutions of such equations must be plane waves: substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, the same factor cancels for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and what remains is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, that is"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mtext>  </mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"demo":"chain"},{"h":"Long waves, short waves and the Brillouin zone"},{"p":"When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is small (wavelength much longer than the spacing), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: frequency proportional to wavevector, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> the speed of sound; here the crystal is just a continuous elastic rod, which is where Debye started in 1912 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-debye1912\" data-ref=\"debye1912\">Debye 1912</a>]</span>. As the wavelength shortens the curve bends over and flattens at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: neighbouring atoms move exactly opposite, the group velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is zero, and the vibration is a standing wave that carries no energy."},{"p":"What happens for larger <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>? Switch on “add a reciprocal-lattice vector” in the demo and you see it: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> give identical atomic displacements, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. On a continuous string shorter waves are something new; in a crystal there is nothing between the atoms, so “wavelengths” shorter than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> mean nothing. Every distinct vibration can therefore be labelled by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the <b>first Brillouin zone</b>."},{"h":"N atoms, N modes"},{"p":"Real crystals are finite. Born and von Kármán used a clever device in 1912: join the chain end to end and demand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (periodic boundary conditions). Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can only be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of them in the first zone. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> atoms have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> degrees of freedom, turned into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> normal modes, no more and no fewer: the change of coordinates is just a new basis. For large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the points lie densely along the dispersion curve. The details of the boundary only affect modes near the surface, and do not change the bulk properties of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> atoms."},{"p":"Add second-neighbour springs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and the dispersion becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: no longer a pure sine, and the maximum need not sit at the boundary. When the dispersion of real metals such as copper is measured by neutron scattering, fits need force constants out to several shells of neighbours <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-svensson1967\" data-ref=\"svensson1967\">Svensson, Brockhouse &amp; Rowe 1967</a>]</span>, yet the look, linear at long waves and flat at the boundary, is the same as for this simplest chain."},{"note":"<b>Why “phonon”.</b> The long-wave end is just sound, so the quanta of these vibrations (chapter 4) are called phonons (from the Greek for “sound”), by analogy with photons, the quanta of light. The name appeared in the 1930s, usually credited to Tamm, and spread through Frenkel’s books.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"The harmonic approximation","p":"The potential is expanded only to second order in displacements, so the equations of motion are linear and solutions superpose. It fails once amplitudes become comparable to bond lengths (chapter 7).","st":"approx"},{"h":"Translation symmetry ⇒ plane waves","p":"When every atom has the same surroundings, the eigensolutions of the linear equations are e^{iqja}: the vibrational version of Bloch’s theorem.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The dispersion relation","p":"For the nearest-neighbour chain ω = 2√(K/m)|sin(qa/2)|: ω = v_s q for long waves, flat with zero group velocity at the boundary.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The first Brillouin zone","p":"q and q + 2π/a are the same vibration, so all modes fit in −π/a &lt; q ≤ π/a.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Periodic boundary conditions","p":"Born–von Kármán: exactly N allowed q for N atoms; bulk properties do not depend on the details of the boundary.","st":"history"},{"h":"Real crystals need several neighbour shells","p":"The measured dispersions of copper and aluminium need several shells of force constants for a good fit, but the qualitative shape matches the nearest-neighbour model.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>u</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Newton’s equation for the nearest-neighbour chain (harmonic approximation)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Plane-wave solution and dispersion relation."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mi>lim</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Group velocity and sound speed."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Allowed wavevectors under periodic boundary conditions."}],"example":{"q":"A chain of atoms has lattice constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nm and sound speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow></semantics></math> m/s. In the nearest-neighbour model, what is the highest vibrational frequency (in THz and meV)?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; the maximum is at the boundary, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5000</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>13</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> rad/s.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> THz; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.582</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mtext>eV</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mo>×</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>13</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.026</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV = 26 meV."],"a":"About 6.4 THz and 26 meV, close to room-temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV. That is why phonons near room temperature are “half classical, half quantum” (chapter 5)."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-debye1912\" data-ref=\"debye1912\">Debye 1912</a> · <a href=\"#ref-svensson1967\" data-ref=\"svensson1967\">Svensson, Brockhouse &amp; Rowe 1967</a>"},"diatomic":{"id":"diatomic","body":[{"p":"In rock salt sodium and chlorine alternate; silicon has two atoms per primitive cell; almost every real crystal has more than one atom per cell. Replace the one-dimensional chain by one with two kinds of atom alternating and you see the most important new thing about cells with several atoms: the vibrations divide into two kinds of quite different character."},{"h":"Two equations, two branches"},{"p":"Let the two atoms in cell <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> have masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and displacements <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; an intra-cell spring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> joins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and an inter-cell spring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> joins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Write one Newton equation for each kind of atom, substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, and you get two linear equations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. For a non-zero solution the determinant must vanish: a quadratic in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> with two roots. They are the eigenvalues of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Hermitian matrix, the <b>dynamical matrix</b>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"Dividing by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> makes the matrix Hermitian (“mass-weighted”), so its eigenvalues are real and its eigenvectors orthogonal; the actual displacements are the eigenvectors divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> again. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the roots are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∓</mo><mi>K</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"demo":"diatomic"},{"h":"Acoustic and optical branches"},{"p":"The <b>acoustic branch</b> (minus sign) has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, with the two atoms moving equally and in the same direction, the whole cell translating: sound, with a speed set by the total mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and the springs. The <b>optical branch</b> (plus sign) is highest at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, with the atoms moving oppositely, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the cell’s centre of mass at rest. If the two kinds of atom carry opposite charges (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Na</mtext><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Cl</mtext><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>) this vibration creates an oscillating electric dipole that can absorb infrared light directly, hence the name “optical”."},{"p":"At the zone boundary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the two branches become two simpler motions: on the acoustic branch only the heavy atoms move and the light ones stay still, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>; on the optical branch only the light atoms move, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>. Between these frequencies no real <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> satisfies the equations: a disturbance at such a frequency decays exponentially in the crystal and cannot propagate. This is the (phonon) <b>gap</b>. The more the masses differ, the wider it is."},{"p":"With equal masses and equal springs the gap closes, and not by accident: the “cell” can then really be halved, the diatomic chain is just the monatomic one, and the so-called optical branch is the monatomic chain’s stretch at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> ± <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> “folded” into the smaller zone. Conversely, making only the springs different (equal masses) also opens a gap: the model of a one-dimensional “dimerised” chain, of the same family as chapter 9’s Peierls instability and the model of topological phonons (the SSH chain)."},{"h":"Counting branches: 3p"},{"p":"In general, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math> atoms per cell and 3 degrees of freedom per atom in three dimensions, the dynamical matrix is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math> frequencies: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math> branches. Three are always acoustic (the whole cell translating in three directions, frequency going to zero at long wavelength) and the remaining <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> optical. Silicon and diamond have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: 3 acoustic and 3 optical branches; by the 1990s these dispersions could be computed from first principles to almost coincide with neutron experiments <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-giannozzi1991\" data-ref=\"giannozzi1991\">Giannozzi et al. 1991</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>One extra thing in ionic crystals.</b> In three-dimensional ionic crystals the optical branch also splits at long wavelength: the ionic displacement of the longitudinal optical (LO) mode creates a macroscopic electric field whose extra restoring force pushes it up, while the transverse optical (TO) mode has none. They obey the Lyddane–Sachs–Teller relation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>O</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>O</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lyddane1941\" data-ref=\"lyddane1941\">Lyddane, Sachs &amp; Teller 1941</a>]</span>, taken up in chapter 6.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"The dynamical matrix","p":"The mass-weighted force constants Fourier-transformed give D(q), Hermitian, with eigenvalues ω² and eigenvectors the polarisations (divide by √m for the real displacements).","st":"proved"},{"h":"The acoustic branch","p":"ω → 0 as q → 0, the cell translating as a whole; there are always 3 in three dimensions.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The optical branch","p":"At q = 0 the atoms of the cell move relative to one another with the centre of mass fixed; in ionic crystals it carries a dipole and absorbs infrared light.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Frequencies at the boundary","p":"For K₁ = K₂ the acoustic branch √(2K/M) is set by the heavy atom alone and the optical √(2K/m) by the light one, with the gap between.","st":"proved"},{"h":"3p branches","p":"A cell of p atoms has 3p branches: 3 acoustic and 3p − 3 optical.","st":"proved"},{"h":"LO–TO splitting","p":"In ionic crystals the long-range Coulomb field pushes the longitudinal optical mode up, obeying the Lyddane–Sachs–Teller relation.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>u</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Equations of motion of the diatomic chain."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>K</mi><msqrt><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two branches for K₁ = K₂ = K."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Frequencies at the boundary and the centre (m &lt; M)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>κ</mi></munder><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>optical</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"At q = 0 the optical branch leaves the centre of mass at rest."}],"example":{"q":"Build an equal-spring diatomic chain with the masses of sodium and chlorine (22.99 and 35.45). What is the ratio of optical to acoustic frequencies at the boundary? By how much does the optical branch fall from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> to the boundary?","steps":["At the boundary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>35.45</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>22.99</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>1.542</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>1.242</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>1.6485</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>1.284</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: a fall of about 22 %."],"a":"The branches differ by 24 % at the boundary, and the optical branch falls by 22 % across the zone. The demo’s “mass ratio of NaCl” preset checks this. Real NaCl is three-dimensional and adds LO–TO splitting, so this is only the part due to mass."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-lyddane1941\" data-ref=\"lyddane1941\">Lyddane, Sachs &amp; Teller 1941</a> · <a href=\"#ref-giannozzi1991\" data-ref=\"giannozzi1991\">Giannozzi et al. 1991</a>"},"bz":{"id":"bz","body":[{"p":"Open almost any paper on phonons and you find the same kind of figure: a horizontal axis marked Γ, X, W, K, Γ, L, frequency vertically, and a few undulating lines. This chapter explains where that figure comes from."},{"h":"Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone"},{"p":"In one dimension <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> are equivalent. A three-dimensional lattice is generated by basis vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; all vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> for every lattice vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> form the <b>reciprocal lattice</b>, with basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math> describe the same vibration. The region of reciprocal space closer to the origin than to any other reciprocal-lattice point is the <b>first Brillouin zone</b> (the reciprocal lattice’s Wigner–Seitz cell). It is a square for the square lattice, a regular hexagon for the triangular lattice and a truncated octahedron for face-centred cubic."},{"p":"The reciprocal lattice is more than a mathematical device: it is the very lattice of X-ray diffraction. Bragg’s condition says the change of wavevector on scattering equals a reciprocal-lattice vector; chapter 6’s neutron scattering is the same, with a phonon taking a share of the momentum."},{"h":"Dynamical matrix and polarisation"},{"p":"In three dimensions the force on atom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi></mrow></semantics></math> due to a displacement of atom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (its index in the cell) along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is given by force constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Multiply by the phase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, sum over all lattice vectors, divide by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, and you have the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math> dynamical matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. For central-force springs it takes a particularly vivid form: each spring pulls only along its bond, contributing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"The eigenvectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> are the <b>polarisations</b>: the directions in which the atoms vibrate. Along high-symmetry directions the polarisation is exactly parallel to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (longitudinal, L) or perpendicular to it (transverse, T). In general directions they mix, and the demo colours the “longitudinal fraction”. In three dimensions there are usually one longitudinal acoustic (LA) and two transverse acoustic (TA) branches; the transverse ones are generally softer, with lower sound speeds."},{"demo":"bz"},{"h":"High-symmetry paths"},{"p":"The dispersion is a set of surfaces over the whole zone and cannot be drawn directly. The convention is to cut along a broken line joining high-symmetry points: Γ is always the zone centre; for the square lattice X is the midpoint of an edge and M a corner; for the triangular lattice M is an edge midpoint and K a corner; for fcc one often uses Γ–X–W–K–Γ–L. At high-symmetry points the group velocity often vanishes and the dispersion has maxima or saddle points, which leave kinks in the density of states (chapter 4). The full dispersions of aluminium and copper were measured branch by branch along these directions with neutrons in the 1960s <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-stedman1966\" data-ref=\"stedman1966\">Stedman &amp; Nilsson 1966</a>; <a href=\"#ref-svensson1967\" data-ref=\"svensson1967\">Svensson, Brockhouse &amp; Rowe 1967</a>]</span>."},{"p":"Set the square lattice’s second-neighbour springs to zero in the demo and the transverse frequency along Γ–X drops to zero: with nearest-neighbour central forces alone the square lattice has no resistance to shear and slumps at a push. Real crystals stay stable through longer-range interactions, bond-angle forces or electronic contributions. A structure with no restoring force, or a negative one, in some direction is the instability of chapter 9."},{"note":"<b>The flexural branch of 2D materials.</b> A single atomic layer such as graphene also vibrates out of plane (the ZA branch). With no out-of-plane restoring force its dispersion goes as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> rather than linearly, so its low-temperature heat capacity and conductivity differ markedly from three-dimensional crystals; the dispersions and thermodynamics of graphite and diamond can be computed accurately from first principles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mounet2005\" data-ref=\"mounet2005\">Mounet &amp; Marzari 2005</a>]</span>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"The reciprocal lattice","p":"a_i·b_j = 2πδ_ij; q and q + G are the same vibration. It is the same reciprocal lattice as in diffraction.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The first Brillouin zone","p":"The reciprocal lattice’s Wigner–Seitz cell: a square, a hexagon, a truncated octahedron (fcc).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Polarisation","p":"The eigenvectors of the dynamical matrix; purely longitudinal or transverse along high-symmetry directions.","st":"proved"},{"h":"High-symmetry paths","p":"Band plots follow broken lines such as Γ–X–M–Γ; high-symmetry points often host maxima and saddle points.","st":"history"},{"h":"Nearest-neighbour central forces are not enough","p":"Without second neighbours the square lattice has zero transverse frequency and is unstable against shear.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The ZA branch of 2D materials","p":"Out-of-plane bending with ω ∝ q², changing low-temperature heat capacity and conductivity.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Reciprocal basis vectors and reciprocal-lattice vectors."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></munder><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The general dynamical matrix (3p × 3p)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The acoustic sum rule: a rigid translation feels no force, so Γ has 3 zero frequencies."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>44</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>cubic, along </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Long-wave limit: sound speeds from elastic constants and density."}],"example":{"q":"The primitive cell of perovskite SrTiO₃ has 5 atoms. How many phonon branches are there? How many zero frequencies at Γ? If the cell is replaced by a 2 × 2 × 2 supercell, how many frequencies appear at Γ?","steps":["Branches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: 3 acoustic and 12 optical. Γ has just 3 zero frequencies (rigid translations in three directions).","The supercell has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></semantics></math> atoms, so Γ has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></semantics></math> frequencies: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></semantics></math> frequencies at the 8 original q points commensurate with the supercell, “folded” onto Γ."],"a":"15 branches with 3 zeros at Γ; the supercell’s Γ point has 120 frequencies. This “folding” underlies chapter 9’s finite-displacement method: a supercell gives exactly only the q commensurate with it."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-stedman1966\" data-ref=\"stedman1966\">Stedman &amp; Nilsson 1966</a> · <a href=\"#ref-svensson1967\" data-ref=\"svensson1967\">Svensson, Brockhouse &amp; Rowe 1967</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mounet2005\" data-ref=\"mounet2005\">Mounet &amp; Marzari 2005</a>"},"quantum":{"id":"quantum","body":[{"p":"The first three chapters were pure classical mechanics. Yet as early as 1907 Einstein pointed out that the heat capacity of solids falls at low temperature, which classical mechanics cannot explain: one must accept, as Planck had for radiation, that vibrational energy comes in portions <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a>]</span>. This chapter puts the normal modes into quantum mechanics."},{"h":"From normal modes to oscillators"},{"p":"In normal coordinates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></semantics></math> labelling the branch) the harmonic crystal’s Hamiltonian splits into a sum of uncoupled oscillators. Quantise each in the textbook way, with creation and annihilation operators <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is the excitation number of mode <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Put differently: the mode holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <b>phonons</b>, each carrying energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <b>crystal momentum</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> creates a phonon and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> destroys one. Any number of phonons can occupy one mode, so they are bosons; they appear and disappear freely, so their total number is not conserved (zero chemical potential)."},{"p":"A phonon is not a “real” particle but a <b>quasiparticle</b>: a way in which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> atoms move together, which ceases to exist outside the crystal. Its momentum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is defined only up to a reciprocal-lattice vector and is not mechanical momentum (the total momentum of all the atoms in a phonon mode is actually zero, except for the acoustic mode at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>). But in collisions and scattering <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is conserved, up to a reciprocal-lattice vector, just like momentum, and that is enough. Magnons, excitons, polarons… are the same kind of thing: see the <a href=\"../../quasiparticles/\">Quasiparticles</a> section."},{"h":"Temperature and zero-point energy"},{"p":"In thermal equilibrium a mode of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> holds on average <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> phonons (Bose–Einstein with zero chemical potential). For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and the mean energy returns to the classical <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and the mode is “frozen”. Even with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> each mode keeps a <b>zero-point energy</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: atoms jiggle even at absolute zero, light ones especially. That helium does not crystallise at atmospheric pressure even at absolute zero is largely due to zero-point motion."},{"h":"The density of states"},{"p":"Thermodynamic quantities are all sums over modes, such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. The sum depends only on frequency, so one can first group every mode in the zone by frequency: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the number of modes between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the <b>phonon density of states</b>. Its integral over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (all the degrees of freedom)."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>s</mi></munder><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"3.0em\">→</mo><mpadded width=\"+0.6em\" lspace=\"0.3em\"><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>ω</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mpadded></mover><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mtext>3 acoustic</mtext></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"demo":"dos"},{"p":"At low frequency only the long-wave part of the acoustic branches contributes, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (3D), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∝</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (2D) or constant (1D): the source of chapter 5’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> law. At higher frequency kinks and divergences appear: wherever the dispersion goes flat (zero group velocity), many modes crowd onto one frequency, at points called <b>van Hove singularities</b>. In one dimension they diverge as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>; in three the density of states stays finite but its derivative jumps at maxima and saddle points, giving kinks. The fcc model in the demo shows them, and the densities of states of real metals (aluminium, copper) look much like it."},{"p":"The density of states is for more than heat capacity. The vibrational entropy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>vib</mtext></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is also an integral over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, and the vibrational entropies of two structures often differ by tenths of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> per atom, enough to change which is more stable at high temperature <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fultz2010\" data-ref=\"fultz2010\">Fultz 2010</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Phonons and electrons"},{"p":"When atoms move the potential felt by the electrons changes, so electrons can emit or absorb phonons: <b>electron–phonon coupling</b>. It makes the resistance of metals rise with temperature, makes semiconductor gaps shift with temperature, and lets two electrons attract each other by exchanging phonons. In 1950 two groups found that the superconducting transition temperature of mercury depends on isotopic mass, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-maxwell1950\" data-ref=\"maxwell1950\">Maxwell 1950</a>; <a href=\"#ref-reynolds1950\" data-ref=\"reynolds1950\">Reynolds et al. 1950</a>]</span>: if the mass of the nuclei affects superconductivity, lattice vibrations must be involved. Seven years later the BCS theory made that attraction the mechanism of pairing <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bcs1957\" data-ref=\"bcs1957\">Bardeen, Cooper &amp; Schrieffer 1957</a>]</span>. Today electron–phonon coupling can be computed from first principles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-giustino2017\" data-ref=\"giustino2017\">Giustino 2017</a>]</span>; hydrogen sulfide under high pressure, superconducting near 200 K <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-drozdov2015\" data-ref=\"drozdov2015\">Drozdov et al. 2015</a>]</span>, is a product of the same mechanism."},{"note":"<b>Phonons one at a time.</b> The quantum nature of phonons shows in more than heat capacity. Couple a superconducting qubit to a bulk acoustic resonator and you can put individual phonon quantum states in and read them out <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chu2017\" data-ref=\"chu2017\">Chu et al. 2017</a>]</span>. In two-dimensional materials <b>chiral phonons</b> carrying angular momentum have also been observed: the atoms circle, and left- and right-handed ones can be excited separately <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zhu2018\" data-ref=\"zhu2018\">Zhu et al. 2018</a>]</span>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"One oscillator per mode","p":"In normal coordinates H = Σ ħω(a†a + ½), with energies ħω(n + ½).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Phonons are bosons with no conserved number","p":"In equilibrium ⟨n⟩ = 1/(e^{ħω/k_BT} − 1), with zero chemical potential.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Crystal momentum is not mechanical momentum","p":"ħq is defined only up to a reciprocal-lattice vector and conserved in collisions only up to one (chapter 8’s umklapp processes).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Zero-point energy","p":"At least ½ħω per mode; zero-point motion of light atoms can affect structures and phase diagrams.","st":"tested"},{"h":"DOS and van Hove singularities","p":"g ∝ ω² at low frequency (3D); where the dispersion goes flat the DOS shows kinks or divergences.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The isotope effect","p":"Mercury’s T_c ∝ M^{−1/2} showed lattice vibrations take part in superconductivity; BCS theory made it phonon-mediated pairing.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Single-phonon quantum states","p":"Superconducting qubits can control individual phonons in mechanical resonators; chiral phonons have been observed in 2D materials.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>s</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><msqrt><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi><mi>m</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Creation and annihilation operators and the displacement operator."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Bose–Einstein distribution (μ = 0)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>coth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Mean-square displacement of one mode: zero-point motion remains at T = 0."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Normalisation of the density of states."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The isotope effect in conventional superconductors."}],"example":{"q":"Silicon’s zone-centre optical phonon is at 520 cm⁻¹. What is its energy in meV? How many phonons does it hold on average at room temperature (300 K)? What is its zero-point energy?","steps":["1 meV = 8.0655 cm⁻¹, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>520</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8.0655</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>64.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV. At 300 K, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25.85</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.49</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2.49</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12.1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.090</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. The zero-point energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV is more than five times the mean thermal energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV."],"a":"64.5 meV, 0.09 phonons on average, and 32 meV of zero-point energy. At room temperature silicon’s optical phonon is mostly in its ground state; chapter 6’s Raman thermometer relies on exactly this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a> · <a href=\"#ref-maxwell1950\" data-ref=\"maxwell1950\">Maxwell 1950</a> · <a href=\"#ref-reynolds1950\" data-ref=\"reynolds1950\">Reynolds et al. 1950</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bcs1957\" data-ref=\"bcs1957\">Bardeen, Cooper &amp; Schrieffer 1957</a> · <a href=\"#ref-fultz2010\" data-ref=\"fultz2010\">Fultz 2010</a> · <a href=\"#ref-drozdov2015\" data-ref=\"drozdov2015\">Drozdov et al. 2015</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chu2017\" data-ref=\"chu2017\">Chu et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-giustino2017\" data-ref=\"giustino2017\">Giustino 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zhu2018\" data-ref=\"zhu2018\">Zhu et al. 2018</a>"},"heat":{"id":"heat","body":[{"p":"In 1819 Dulong and Petit found that many solid elements have a molar heat capacity of about 25 J/(mol·K). In classical statistical mechanics this is natural: each atom is a three-dimensional oscillator, kinetic and potential energy each contributing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> per direction, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> per atom and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24.94</mn></mrow></semantics></math> J/(mol·K) per mole. But diamond at room temperature has only about 6 J/(mol·K), roughly a quarter of this, and every solid’s heat capacity goes to zero on cooling. It was one of the earliest pieces of evidence for quantum mechanics."},{"h":"Einstein: one frequency"},{"p":"Einstein’s 1907 model is the simplest: every atom is a three-dimensional quantum oscillator of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, all independent <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a>]</span>. With the previous chapter’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math> each mode has mean energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, and differentiating with respect to temperature gives"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"At high temperature, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, it returns to Dulong–Petit; at low temperature it falls exponentially as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Einstein used it to fit diamond, qualitatively quite right. But careful low-temperature measurements fall much more slowly than exponentially, as a power law. The reason is that Einstein ignored dispersion: the atoms are connected, so there are always long-wave acoustic phonons of arbitrarily low frequency that any temperature can excite."},{"h":"Debye: the elastic continuum"},{"p":"In 1912 Debye treated the crystal as an elastic continuum with all acoustic branches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, cut off at a maximum frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> so that there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> modes in all (in effect replacing the Brillouin zone by a sphere of the same volume) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-debye1912\" data-ref=\"debye1912\">Debye 1912</a>]</span>. With the Debye temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, the heat capacity is"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"3.0em\">→</mo><mpadded width=\"+0.6em\" lspace=\"0.3em\"><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>T</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mpadded></mover><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"demo":"cv"},{"p":"The low-temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> law depends only on long-wave phonons, that is only on the elastic constants, so it is exact: at low enough temperature the lattice heat capacity of any three-dimensional insulating crystal goes as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. (Metals have an extra electronic term proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> that wins at the lowest temperatures.) At intermediate temperatures the Debye model is only an approximation: real densities of states have van Hove kinks and optical branches, so the “Debye temperature” often varies with temperature, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> from low-temperature heat capacity and from elastic constants are not quite equal. The demo puts the Debye curve beside the heat capacity computed from the fcc model’s true density of states."},{"h":"The Debye temperature as a yardstick"},{"p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> roughly tells you “how cold counts as cold”. Lead is 105 K and long classical at room temperature; copper 343 K and aluminium 428 K are nearly at Dulong–Petit at room temperature; silicon 645 K; diamond 2230 K, far from fully excited at room temperature. Light atoms and stiff bonds give high <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, the same thing as chapter 1’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> turns up elsewhere too: the Bloch–Grüneisen formula for resistivity, the Lindemann criterion for melting, and the Debye–Waller factor of X-ray diffraction."},{"p":"Experiments usually measure the constant-pressure heat capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, which exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>B</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, a term due to thermal expansion (chapter 7). At high temperature this lifts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and that excess, plus anharmonic effects, is the main reason Dulong–Petit is only approximate. Vibrational free energy, entropy, thermal expansion and the temperature dependence of elasticity all follow from the same density of states; this “vibrational thermodynamics” matters for the phase stability of materials <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fultz2010\" data-ref=\"fultz2010\">Fultz 2010</a>]</span>, and these properties of graphite and diamond can be computed from first principles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mounet2005\" data-ref=\"mounet2005\">Mounet &amp; Marzari 2005</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>Link to Heat, Statistics and Flow.</b> The derivation here is the canonical ensemble of statistical mechanics: the partition function is a product of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sinh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> over modes, and free energy, entropy and heat capacity are its derivatives. Statistical mechanics itself is in <a href=\"../../macro/#c-statmech\">Heat, Statistics and Flow · statistical mechanics</a>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Dulong–Petit","p":"Classical equipartition: 3k_B per atom, 3R ≈ 24.9 J/(mol·K). Roughly true only for T ≳ Θ_D.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The Einstein model","p":"Quantum oscillators of one frequency: explains the fall, but falls too fast at low T (exponential, not a power law).","st":"approx"},{"h":"Debye’s T³ law","p":"At low T only long-wave acoustic phonons count, and C ∝ T³ is exact for 3D insulators.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The Debye temperature","p":"Light atoms and stiff bonds give high Θ_D: lead 105 K, copper 343 K, silicon 645 K, diamond 2230 K.","st":"tested"},{"h":"C_p and C_V","p":"C_p − C_V = TVα²B, due to thermal expansion; it lifts C_p above 3R at high T.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Vibrational thermodynamics","p":"Free energy and entropy follow from the DOS, and vibrational entropy can decide which phase is stable at high T.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sinh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sinh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Partition function and free energy of a harmonic crystal."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Heat capacity for any density of states."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mi>V</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Debye frequency from number density and the mean sound speed."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1944</mn><mtext> </mtext><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The low-temperature limit (per mole of atoms)."}],"example":{"q":"Copper has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>343</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K. In the Debye model, what is its molar heat capacity at 10 K and at 300 K?","steps":["10 K is far below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so use the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> law: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1944</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>343</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1944</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2.48</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.048</mn></mrow></semantics></math> J/(mol·K). (Measured copper at 10 K also has an electronic term of similar size.)","At 300 K, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.14</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and the full Debye integral gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.938</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>23.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> J/(mol·K). Pick copper in the demo and drag to 300 K to check."],"a":"0.048 and 23.4 J/(mol·K). The measured <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of copper at 298 K is about 24.4 J/(mol·K); the small difference is mostly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and the electronic heat capacity."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a> · <a href=\"#ref-debye1912\" data-ref=\"debye1912\">Debye 1912</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mounet2005\" data-ref=\"mounet2005\">Mounet &amp; Marzari 2005</a> · <a href=\"#ref-fultz2010\" data-ref=\"fultz2010\">Fultz 2010</a>"},"measure":{"id":"measure","body":[{"p":"The dispersion curves and densities of states of the first five chapters are not just theory: from the 1950s on they were measured, branch by branch. Every measurement rests on one thing: a particle (neutron, photon) enters the crystal, creates or absorbs a phonon, and comes out with changed energy and momentum."},{"h":"Two conservation laws"},{"p":"Let the incoming particle have energy and wavevector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and the outgoing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. When it creates a phonon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (the “Stokes” side):"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"For absorbing a phonon the signs flip. The reciprocal-lattice vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in the second equation means the scattering vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can lie in any Brillouin zone, and what is measured is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> folded back into the first. Creation and absorption have intensities proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: at low temperature there are almost no phonons to absorb and only the Stokes side remains. Their ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is <b>detailed balance</b>."},{"h":"Inelastic neutron scattering"},{"p":"A neutron’s energy and wavevector obey <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>meV</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2.072</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>A</mtext><mo>˚</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. “Thermal” neutrons moderated to room temperature in a reactor have about 25 meV and a wavelength near 1.8 Å: phonon energies and interatomic wavelengths at once. So a neutron can knock out a phonon of any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, with an energy change large enough to measure well. Brockhouse invented the <b>triple-axis spectrometer</b> (choose the incident energy, rotate the sample to choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, choose the final energy) and in 1955 measured phonons in aluminium <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1955\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1955\">Brockhouse &amp; Stewart 1955</a>]</span>; the complete dispersions of aluminium and copper were later measured this way <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-stedman1966\" data-ref=\"stedman1966\">Stedman &amp; Nilsson 1966</a>; <a href=\"#ref-svensson1967\" data-ref=\"svensson1967\">Svensson, Brockhouse &amp; Rowe 1967</a>]</span>. He shared the 1994 Nobel Prize in Physics with Shull for it <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1995\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1995\">Brockhouse 1995</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"scatter"},{"p":"Fixing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and scanning the energy transfer in the demo is a “constant-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> scan”: a peak at each of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, with heights set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. With too low an incident energy some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> are kinematically unreachable (the neutron has not got that much energy to give), the first thing to work out when planning an experiment. Neutrons have two further strengths: they interact with nuclei, so they are just as sensitive to light elements (hydrogen!); and incoherent scattering gives the (weighted) density of states directly, widely used for vibrational spectra of molecular crystals and hydrogen-bearing materials; see <a href=\"../../spectra/#m-ins\">Spectra Hall · inelastic neutron scattering</a>."},{"h":"Inelastic X-ray scattering"},{"p":"X-rays also have Å wavelengths, but their photon energies are keV: resolving changes of a few meV needs a resolution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Only synchrotrons with very high-order crystal backscattering can do it, and the first phonon dispersion was measured that way in 1987 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dorner1987\" data-ref=\"dorner1987\">Dorner et al. 1987</a>]</span>. The gain is a tiny beam (micrograms of sample, or a sample inside a high-pressure diamond anvil cell) and no neutron-style kinematic limits; the price is that X-rays interact mainly with electrons and are insensitive to light elements <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-burkel2000\" data-ref=\"burkel2000\">Burkel 2000</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Raman scattering"},{"p":"Visible light has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> Å⁻¹, while the Brillouin zone is of order 1 Å⁻¹. Scattering can hand over at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so first-order Raman (one phonon) sees only optical phonons at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Raman and Krishnan discovered this “new type of secondary radiation” in liquids in 1928 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-raman1928\" data-ref=\"raman1928\">Raman &amp; Krishnan 1928</a>]</span>, and today silicon’s 520 cm⁻¹ line is the standard used to calibrate Raman spectrometers. Its position and width shift with temperature, stress and doping <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hart1970\" data-ref=\"hart1970\">Hart, Aggarwal &amp; Lax 1970</a>]</span>, which makes Raman an everyday tool of materials characterisation; see <a href=\"../../spectra/#m-raman\">Spectra Hall · Raman spectroscopy</a>."},{"demo":"raman"},{"p":"The anti-Stokes (absorbing a phonon) to Stokes (creating one) intensity ratio is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: the first factor comes from the cross-section’s dependence on light frequency (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> of dipole radiation; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> when the detector counts photons) and the second is detailed balance. Measure the ratio and you can infer the sample’s local temperature, which is how one estimates how much the laser has heated it."},{"h":"Infrared absorption and LO–TO splitting"},{"p":"Infrared light couples directly to dipoles, so the transverse optical phonons of ionic crystals absorb it strongly. Between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> the dielectric function is negative, light cannot enter and is almost totally reflected: the <b>reststrahlen band</b>. The two frequencies are linked by the Lyddane–Sachs–Teller relation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lyddane1941\" data-ref=\"lyddane1941\">Lyddane, Sachs &amp; Teller 1941</a>]</span>. Getting LO–TO splitting right in first-principles calculations needs Born effective charges and the high-frequency dielectric constant, which density-functional perturbation theory provides <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gonze1997\" data-ref=\"gonze1997\">Gonze &amp; Lee 1997</a>]</span>. Infrared and Raman selection rules follow from crystal symmetry: in a centrosymmetric crystal no mode is both infrared and Raman active. See <a href=\"../../spectra/#m-ir\">Spectra Hall · infrared spectroscopy</a>."}],"points":[{"h":"Energy and crystal momentum both conserved","p":"E_i − E_f = ħω and k_i − k_f = q + G; creation and absorption differ by e^{−ħω/k_BT}.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Thermal neutrons fit perfectly","p":"About 25 meV and 1.8 Å: energy and wavelength both comparable to phonons, so any q can be measured.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The triple-axis spectrometer","p":"Brockhouse measured phonons in aluminium in 1955; complete dispersions followed branch by branch.","st":"history"},{"h":"Inelastic X-ray scattering","p":"meV resolution at synchrotrons; tiny samples and high pressure are fine, but light elements are hard to see.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Raman sees only Γ","p":"Light’s wavevector is about a thousandth of the zone, so first-order Raman sees only q ≈ 0.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The Raman thermometer","p":"The anti-Stokes to Stokes ratio ≈ (frequency factor) × e^{−hcν/k_BT}.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The reststrahlen band","p":"Ionic crystals reflect infrared almost totally between ω_TO and ω_LO; ω_LO/ω_TO follows from the LST relation.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0721</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>A</mtext><mo>˚</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>81.80</mn><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>A</mtext><mo>˚</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Energy, wavevector and wavelength of a neutron."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∝</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>s</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The one-phonon scattering function (monatomic crystal, Debye–Waller factor omitted)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Raman anti-Stokes to Stokes intensity ratio."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>O</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>O</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Lyddane–Sachs–Teller relation."}],"example":{"q":"Silicon’s 520 cm⁻¹ line is measured with a 532 nm laser at room temperature (300 K). What should the anti-Stokes to Stokes ratio be? If 0.15 is measured, roughly what is the sample temperature?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>532</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18797</mn></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻¹. The frequency factor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>18797</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>520</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>18797</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>520</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0569</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.248</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.4388</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>520</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>300</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.494</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.494</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0826</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.248</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.0826</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.103</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Inverting, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.4388</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>520</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.248</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>748.2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.119</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>353</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K."],"a":"About 0.10 at room temperature; a measured 0.15 means the laser has heated the spot to about 350 K. Drag the demo’s temperature slider to compare the two frequency factors (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>)."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-raman1928\" data-ref=\"raman1928\">Raman &amp; Krishnan 1928</a> · <a href=\"#ref-lyddane1941\" data-ref=\"lyddane1941\">Lyddane, Sachs &amp; Teller 1941</a> · <a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1955\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1955\">Brockhouse &amp; Stewart 1955</a> · <a href=\"#ref-stedman1966\" data-ref=\"stedman1966\">Stedman &amp; Nilsson 1966</a> · <a href=\"#ref-svensson1967\" data-ref=\"svensson1967\">Svensson, Brockhouse &amp; Rowe 1967</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hart1970\" data-ref=\"hart1970\">Hart, Aggarwal &amp; Lax 1970</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dorner1987\" data-ref=\"dorner1987\">Dorner et al. 1987</a> · <a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1995\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1995\">Brockhouse 1995</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gonze1997\" data-ref=\"gonze1997\">Gonze &amp; Lee 1997</a> · <a href=\"#ref-burkel2000\" data-ref=\"burkel2000\">Burkel 2000</a>"},"anharm":{"id":"anharm","body":[{"p":"A harmonic crystal has some unrealistic properties: two phonons pass through each other untouched; heating makes the atoms swing harder but leaves their mean positions alone; a heat current, once started, never decays. All of these are fixed by the higher-order terms dropped from the expansion of the potential:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"h":"Thermal expansion and the quasi-harmonic approximation"},{"p":"A real bond is easier to stretch than to compress (the potential curve is asymmetric and the cubic term non-zero), so the larger the amplitude, the longer the mean bond: thermal expansion. A cheap and often very good treatment is the <b>quasi-harmonic approximation</b>: at each volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the crystal is still treated as harmonic, but the frequencies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> depend on volume; then for each temperature find the volume minimising the free energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>vib</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. How fast the frequencies change with volume is given by the <b>Grüneisen parameter</b> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-grueneisen1912\" data-ref=\"grueneisen1912\">Grüneisen 1912</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"demo":"qha"},{"p":"The second equation is the Grüneisen relation: thermal expansion is proportional to heat capacity, so it also vanishes as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> at low temperature and levels off at high temperature. For most materials <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is between 1 and 3. If some modes have negative <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (softening when compressed, common for transverse rocking in framework structures with “hinges”) and they dominate the expansion, the crystal shrinks on heating. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>ZrW</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mtext>O</mtext><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> has negative thermal expansion all the way from 0.3 K to 1050 K <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mary1996\" data-ref=\"mary1996\">Mary et al. 1996</a>]</span>. The quasi-harmonic approximation fails near melting or with strongly anharmonic modes, because its premise, harmonic at each volume, is no longer right."},{"h":"Phonon lifetimes"},{"p":"The cubic term lets one phonon decay into two (or two merge into one), as long as energy is conserved and crystal momentum is conserved up to a reciprocal-lattice vector. Each phonon thus has a finite lifetime <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, its line a width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and its frequency a small shift. Raman peaks broadening and moving down with temperature is this effect (last chapter’s silicon line). Lifetimes of every phonon across the zone can now be computed from first principles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-togo2015b\" data-ref=\"togo2015b\">Togo, Chaput &amp; Tanaka 2015</a>]</span>, and they are the raw material for the next chapter’s thermal conductivity."},{"h":"Soft modes and structural phase transitions"},{"p":"In some crystals the high-symmetry structure is really unstable at low temperature: in the harmonic approximation some mode has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, and only anharmonic fluctuations at high temperature hold it up. On cooling that mode’s frequency drops, reaches zero at the transition, and the crystal distorts along the mode into a lower-symmetry structure. In 1960 Cochran explained ferroelectric transitions (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>BaTiO</mtext><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>PbTiO</mtext><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, for instance) as the softening of a transverse optical mode <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cochran1960\" data-ref=\"cochran1960\">Cochran 1960</a>]</span>, and later neutron and Raman experiments found soft modes in many perovskites."},{"p":"Here the quasi-harmonic approximation cannot even produce a stable high-temperature phase, and genuinely anharmonic methods are needed: fit the atomic motion in simulations to temperature-dependent “effective” force constants (TDEP, <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hellman2011\" data-ref=\"hellman2011\">Hellman, Abrikosov &amp; Simak 2011</a>]</span>), or treat anharmonicity and quantum fluctuations together variationally with the stochastic self-consistent harmonic approximation (SSCHA). The latter explained palladium hydride’s “inverse isotope effect”, in which the deuteride superconducts at a higher temperature than the hydride <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-errea2013\" data-ref=\"errea2013\">Errea, Calandra &amp; Mauri 2013</a>]</span>."},{"h":"FPU: a surprising computer experiment"},{"p":"From 1953 to 1955 Fermi, Pasta, Ulam and Mary Tsingou, who did the programming, simulated a chain of atoms with weak cubic (or quartic) nonlinearity on the MANIAC computer at Los Alamos. They put all the energy into the lowest normal mode, expecting nonlinearity to spread it slowly over every mode (the equipartition of statistical mechanics). Instead the energy wandered among the first few modes and, after a while, came almost entirely back to the first <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauxois2008\" data-ref=\"dauxois2008\">Dauxois 2008</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"fpu"},{"p":"This “FPU recurrence” later acquired several layers of explanation: Zabusky and Kruskal derived the KdV equation in the continuum limit and found solitary waves that pass through one another unchanged, naming them “solitons” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zabusky1965\" data-ref=\"zabusky1965\">Zabusky &amp; Kruskal 1965</a>]</span>; the KAM theorem says most orbits of a weakly perturbed integrable system remain on invariant tori; and with enough energy and enough time the chain does thermalise, though the time scale can be enormous <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-berman2005\" data-ref=\"berman2005\">Berman &amp; Izrailev 2005</a>]</span>. The demo’s “β chain, high energy” mixes noticeably faster."}],"points":[{"h":"Anharmonic terms","p":"Cubic terms give thermal expansion and three-phonon scattering; quartic terms give temperature-dependent frequencies and four-phonon scattering.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The quasi-harmonic approximation","p":"Harmonic at each volume with volume-dependent frequencies, minimise the free energy: the standard method for thermal expansion, failing near melting or under strong anharmonicity.","st":"approx"},{"h":"The Grüneisen relation","p":"α = γC_V/(BV): thermal expansion tracks heat capacity.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Negative thermal expansion","p":"When negative-γ modes dominate the crystal shrinks on heating; ZrW₂O₈ does so from 0.3 K to 1050 K.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Lifetimes and linewidths","p":"Three-phonon processes give every phonon a finite lifetime, which first principles can compute one by one.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Soft modes","p":"A mode whose frequency drops to zero with temperature drives a structural transition (a ferroelectric one, say).","st":"tested"},{"h":"The FPU recurrence","p":"A weakly nonlinear chain does not quickly share its energy and the energy nearly all returns; with long times and high energies it eventually thermalises.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The quasi-harmonic free energy."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Mode Grüneisen parameters and thermal expansion (c is each mode’s heat capacity)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Conservation in three-phonon processes (G = 0 is a normal process)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The FPU chain potential."}],"example":{"q":"Copper has a Grüneisen parameter of about 2.0, bulk modulus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>140</mn></mrow></semantics></math> GPa, molar volume 7.11 cm³/mol and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>23.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> J/(mol·K) at 300 K (chapter 5). Estimate its linear expansion coefficient.","steps":["Heat capacity per volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>23.4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7.11</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3.29</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> J/(m³·K).","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3.29</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>11</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4.7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> K⁻¹; the linear coefficient is a third of that, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> K⁻¹."],"a":"About <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> K⁻¹, against a measured <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> K⁻¹. One parameter ties heat capacity, stiffness and thermal expansion together."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-grueneisen1912\" data-ref=\"grueneisen1912\">Grüneisen 1912</a> · <a href=\"#ref-cochran1960\" data-ref=\"cochran1960\">Cochran 1960</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zabusky1965\" data-ref=\"zabusky1965\">Zabusky &amp; Kruskal 1965</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mary1996\" data-ref=\"mary1996\">Mary et al. 1996</a> · <a href=\"#ref-berman2005\" data-ref=\"berman2005\">Berman &amp; Izrailev 2005</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dauxois2008\" data-ref=\"dauxois2008\">Dauxois 2008</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hellman2011\" data-ref=\"hellman2011\">Hellman, Abrikosov &amp; Simak 2011</a> · <a href=\"#ref-errea2013\" data-ref=\"errea2013\">Errea, Calandra &amp; Mauri 2013</a> · <a href=\"#ref-togo2015b\" data-ref=\"togo2015b\">Togo, Chaput &amp; Tanaka 2015</a>"},"transport":{"id":"transport","body":[{"p":"Diamond feels cold to the touch; glass does not. Neither conducts electricity, yet their thermal conductivities differ a thousandfold: diamond is about 2000 W/(m·K) at room temperature, four or five times copper; fused silica about 1.4 W/(m·K). This chapter is about how phonons carry heat and what gets in their way."},{"h":"The phonon gas"},{"p":"A phonon in wave-packet form moves at the group velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></msub><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> carrying energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. A temperature gradient leaves more phonons on the hot side than the cold, and their net flow is the heat current. The mean distance between collisions is the mean free path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Kinetic theory gives the simplest estimate; more accurately one sums over modes:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>v</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⟶</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>V</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>β</mi></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"Each mode’s lifetime <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is the key. There are three sources of scattering: the sample’s <b>boundaries</b> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, independent of frequency), <b>point defects</b> and isotopes (atoms of different mass act as small obstacles, Rayleigh scattering <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>), and collisions <b>between phonons</b> (the previous chapter’s three-phonon processes, increasing with temperature)."},{"h":"Umklapp processes"},{"p":"In 1929 Peierls noticed something subtle <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-peierls1929\" data-ref=\"peierls1929\">Peierls 1929</a>]</span>: if two phonons merge into one with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (a <b>normal process</b>, N process), the total crystal momentum of the phonons is unchanged and so is the direction of the heat current: the gas merely redistributes its energy and keeps flowing, with no thermal resistance. Only <b>umklapp processes</b> (German for “flipping over”, U processes), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, flip the momentum back and break up the current. Umklapp needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> to reach outside the zone, so the phonons involved need frequencies of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; at low temperature these thin out as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, which is why the conductivity of pure crystals rises steeply on cooling."},{"demo":"kappa"},{"p":"Put together, conductivity has three regimes in temperature: at the lowest temperatures phonons are few and the mean free path is limited only by the boundaries, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∝</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>; at some intermediate temperature there is a peak whose height depends on how pure and how large the sample is; at high temperature umklapp dominates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∝</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. In 1959 Callaway added these scattering rates up as functions of frequency in a model still widely used <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-callaway1959\" data-ref=\"callaway1959\">Callaway 1959</a>]</span>; the demo is a toy version along those lines. In alloys point-defect scattering is strong and the peak is flattened: just what thermoelectric materials want (conduct electricity, not heat)."},{"h":"Thermal conductivity from first principles"},{"p":"From 2007 the lattice thermal conductivity of semiconductors could be computed with no fitted parameters at all: density functional theory for the second- and third-order force constants, giving every phonon’s group velocity and three-phonon scattering rate, then a solution of the linearised phonon Boltzmann equation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-broido2007\" data-ref=\"broido2007\">Broido et al. 2007</a>]</span>. Programs such as ShengBTE and phono3py made this a routine calculation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-li2014\" data-ref=\"li2014\">Li et al. 2014</a>; <a href=\"#ref-togo2015b\" data-ref=\"togo2015b\">Togo, Chaput &amp; Tanaka 2015</a>]</span>, with results for common materials such as silicon, germanium and diamond within ten or twenty per cent of experiment <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lindsay2018\" data-ref=\"lindsay2018\">Lindsay et al. 2018</a>]</span>."},{"p":"The best example is cubic boron arsenide. A 2013 calculation predicted a room-temperature conductivity rivalling diamond <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lindsay2013\" data-ref=\"lindsay2013\">Lindsay, Broido &amp; Reinecke 2013</a>]</span>: arsenic is heavy and boron light, so a wide gap separates the acoustic and optical branches, two acoustic phonons can hardly merge into an optical one, and three-phonon scattering is strongly suppressed. Adding four-phonon scattering later lowered the prediction to about 1300 W/(m·K); in 2018 three groups measured roughly 1000 to 1300 W/(m·K) on high-quality crystals <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kang2018\" data-ref=\"kang2018\">Kang et al. 2018</a>; <a href=\"#ref-li2018\" data-ref=\"li2018\">Li et al. 2018</a>; <a href=\"#ref-tian2018\" data-ref=\"tian2018\">Tian et al. 2018</a>]</span>. A fine case of “computed first, found afterwards”. Single-layer graphene also conducts heat extremely well: the first Raman-based measurement gave thousands of W/(m·K) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-balandin2008\" data-ref=\"balandin2008\">Balandin et al. 2008</a>]</span>, with the exact numbers much discussed since."},{"h":"Beyond the phonon gas"},{"p":"The gas picture fails in two limits. At one extreme normal processes vastly outnumber umklapp: phonons flow together like a fluid (phonon hydrodynamics), and heat can propagate as a wave, <b>second sound</b>. It used to be seen only in ultrapure crystals at a few kelvin; in 2019 it was observed in graphite above 100 K <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huberman2019\" data-ref=\"huberman2019\">Huberman et al. 2019</a>]</span>. At the other extreme are glasses and strongly disordered materials: vibrations no longer have a definite wavevector, the mean free path is shorter than the wavelength, the “particle” picture loses its meaning, and heat passes by coupling between neighbouring modes (“diffusons”). In 2019 Simoncelli, Marzari and Mauri wrote down a transport equation unifying crystals and glasses <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-simoncelli2019\" data-ref=\"simoncelli2019\">Simoncelli, Marzari &amp; Mauri 2019</a>]</span>. Machine-learned foundation potentials are also starting to be used for thermal conductivity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pota2024\" data-ref=\"pota2024\">Póta et al. 2024</a>]</span> (see the next chapter)."}],"points":[{"h":"κ = ⅓Cvℓ","p":"Kinetic theory of the phonon gas; the accurate version sums c v² τ over modes.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Only umklapp produces resistance","p":"Normal processes keep the total phonon crystal momentum and do not degrade the current; umklapp, boundaries and defects do.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The conductivity peak","p":"T³ at low T (boundaries), 1/T at high T (umklapp), a peak in between whose height depends on purity and size.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Alloys and thermoelectrics","p":"ω⁴ point-defect scattering lowers conductivity, one lever in thermoelectric design.","st":"tested"},{"h":"First-principles conductivity","p":"Second- and third-order force constants plus the phonon Boltzmann equation; within 10–20 % of experiment for common semiconductors.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Boron arsenide","p":"Predicted by calculation, measured in 2018 at about 1000–1300 W/(m·K).","st":"tested"},{"h":"Second sound and glasses","p":"Phonon hydrodynamics and transport in disordered materials both go beyond the simple phonon gas and are still developing.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Group velocity."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>T</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Callaway-style scattering rates: boundaries, point defects, umklapp (one common form)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Thermal conductivity integral in the Debye model."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Normal and umklapp processes."}],"example":{"q":"Silicon’s room-temperature conductivity is about 150 W/(m·K), its heat capacity per volume about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.66</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> J/(m³·K), and take a mean sound speed of 6400 m/s. From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, what is the mean free path? What does that mean for nanoscale devices?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>150</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.66</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>6400</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m = 42 nm.","This “average” misleads: the heat capacity comes mostly from high-frequency phonons (with short free paths), while much of the heat is carried by low-frequency acoustic phonons whose free paths, first-principles calculations show, reach microns. So silicon structures of tens to hundreds of nanometres already conduct noticeably less than the bulk."],"a":"A simple estimate gives about 40 nm, but the real distribution is broad, with heat-carrying phonons reaching microns. Nanowire and thin-film conductivities therefore depend on size: the demo’s “nanowire” is exactly this."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-peierls1929\" data-ref=\"peierls1929\">Peierls 1929</a> · <a href=\"#ref-callaway1959\" data-ref=\"callaway1959\">Callaway 1959</a> · <a href=\"#ref-broido2007\" data-ref=\"broido2007\">Broido et al. 2007</a> · <a href=\"#ref-balandin2008\" data-ref=\"balandin2008\">Balandin et al. 2008</a> · <a href=\"#ref-lindsay2013\" data-ref=\"lindsay2013\">Lindsay, Broido &amp; Reinecke 2013</a> · <a href=\"#ref-li2014\" data-ref=\"li2014\">Li et al. 2014</a> · <a href=\"#ref-togo2015b\" data-ref=\"togo2015b\">Togo, Chaput &amp; Tanaka 2015</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kang2018\" data-ref=\"kang2018\">Kang et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-li2018\" data-ref=\"li2018\">Li et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-lindsay2018\" data-ref=\"lindsay2018\">Lindsay et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-tian2018\" data-ref=\"tian2018\">Tian et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-huberman2019\" data-ref=\"huberman2019\">Huberman et al. 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-simoncelli2019\" data-ref=\"simoncelli2019\">Simoncelli, Marzari &amp; Mauri 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-pota2024\" data-ref=\"pota2024\">Póta et al. 2024</a>"},"compute":{"id":"compute","body":[{"p":"Every chapter so far assumed we know the force constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. For a new material, where do they come from? Once from fits to neutron data; today almost always from quantum mechanics. This chapter is about how to compute them and where things go wrong."},{"h":"Finite displacements (frozen phonons)"},{"p":"Force constants are second derivatives of the energy with respect to displacements, or first derivatives of the forces: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>κ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>κ</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. The most direct algorithm: take an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> supercell, move one atom by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math> along one direction (typically around 0.01 Å), compute the forces on every atom once with density functional theory and divide by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; central differences (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, one calculation each) cancel the first-order error. Crystal symmetry greatly reduces the number of displacements needed. Parlinski and co-workers used this approach in 1997 to compute the soft mode of cubic ZrO₂ <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-parlinski1997\" data-ref=\"parlinski1997\">Parlinski, Li &amp; Kawazoe 1997</a>]</span>, and phonopy, today’s most widely used program, works the same way <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-togo2015\" data-ref=\"togo2015\">Togo &amp; Tanaka 2015</a>; <a href=\"#ref-togo2023\" data-ref=\"togo2023\">Togo 2023</a>]</span>."},{"p":"The supercell decides which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> come out exactly: those commensurate with it (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of them for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> cells) are exact and the rest come from Fourier interpolation. How good the interpolation is depends on how fast the force constants decay: short-range covalent and metallic forces decay quickly and interpolate well; the Coulomb forces of ionic crystals are long-range and need a separate “non-analytic correction” (using Born effective charges and the dielectric constant), without which the LO–TO splitting does not appear."},{"demo":"frozen"},{"p":"The demo’s chain has Morse bonds plus a repulsive tail of adjustable range. A few things become clear at once: with a long range and a small supercell, interpolation goes badly wrong at non-commensurate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; too large a displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math> lets anharmonic terms creep in; too small an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math> amplifies numerical noise in the forces by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; and stretching the chain past the inflection point of the potential makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> negative at the zone boundary: an <b>imaginary frequency</b>."},{"h":"Real and spurious imaginary frequencies"},{"p":"An imaginary frequency means the energy curves downward along that mode and the structure is unstable in that direction. It may be real: a high-temperature phase that is genuinely unstable at zero temperature (last chapter’s soft modes), calling for anharmonic methods. Or spurious: a structure not relaxed to a true minimum, too small a supercell, insufficiently accurate forces (coarse integration grids, too few <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> points), or a violated acoustic sum rule (a little imaginary frequency near Γ). To tell them apart, enlarge the supercell and tighten the settings to see whether it goes away, and actually displace the atoms along the mode to see whether the energy drops."},{"h":"Density-functional perturbation theory"},{"p":"The other route needs no supercell: treat a periodic displacement of wavevector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> as a perturbation on the electrons and compute the first-order change in density and the force constants directly by linear response <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-baroni1987\" data-ref=\"baroni1987\">Baroni, Giannozzi &amp; Testa 1987</a>]</span>. Any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> costs about as much as one primitive cell, and Born effective charges, dielectric tensors and electron–phonon coupling come along too <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gonze1997\" data-ref=\"gonze1997\">Gonze &amp; Lee 1997</a>; <a href=\"#ref-baroni2001\" data-ref=\"baroni2001\">Baroni et al. 2001</a>]</span>. First-principles dispersions of semiconductors already almost coincided with neutron experiments in 1991 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-giannozzi1991\" data-ref=\"giannozzi1991\">Giannozzi et al. 1991</a>]</span>. Programs such as Quantum ESPRESSO and ABINIT implement it; finite displacements have the advantage of working with any method that can produce forces."},{"h":"Machine-learned potentials"},{"p":"“Any method that can produce forces” includes machine-learned interatomic potentials. Finite displacements need only accurate forces, and a trained potential computes them several orders of magnitude faster than density functional theory, so large supercells, higher-order force constants and anharmonic effects from molecular dynamics all become cheap. Equivariant graph networks (NequIP <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>, MACE <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batatia2022\" data-ref=\"batatia2022\">Batatia et al. 2022</a>]</span>) do particularly well on second derivatives, because their energies are smooth in the coordinates and have the right symmetries; see <a href=\"../../nequip/#c-forces\">NequIP · forces and energy conservation</a>. Universal potentials trained on many materials (CHGNet <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-deng2023\" data-ref=\"deng2023\">Deng et al. 2023</a>]</span>, for instance) can compute phonons with no dedicated training; a 2025 systematic test against a large set of DFT phonon results found the best universal models already fairly accurate for most materials, but with clear systematic biases (overall softening is common) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-loew2025\" data-ref=\"loew2025\">Loew et al. 2025</a>]</span>. Using them for the harder problem of thermal conductivity is still being tested <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pota2024\" data-ref=\"pota2024\">Póta et al. 2024</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>A shortest path from structure to phonons.</b> The site’s <a href=\"../../code/\">Code Desk</a> reads XYZ, writes inputs for programs such as PySCF and ORCA and reads frequencies back; sending a structure to the online models (UMA, MACE, xTB) gives molecular vibrational frequencies directly. Phonon dispersions of crystals need a program such as phonopy: the Code Desk’s <a href=\"../../code/#phonon\">crystal phonons</a> tab hands a crystal (a preset, or POSCAR / CIF) to phonopy on the site owner’s computer, with forces from UMA’s inorganic-materials task or EMT, and returns the dispersion along the high-symmetry path, the density of states, the heat capacity and a verdict on imaginary modes; it also gives a script that runs the same steps on your own computer.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Force constants = derivatives of forces","p":"Finite displacement: move one atom, compute forces once, divide by h; central differences cancel the first-order error.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Supercells and interpolation","p":"Only q commensurate with the supercell are exact; interpolation fails when forces reach beyond half the supercell.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The non-analytic correction","p":"Long-range Coulomb forces in ionic crystals need separate treatment with Born effective charges and the dielectric constant to give LO–TO splitting.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Choosing the displacement","p":"Too large an h mixes in anharmonicity, too small amplifies noise; around 0.01 Å is the usual compromise.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Imaginary frequencies","p":"Either a real instability (soft mode) or inadequate settings; distinguish by enlarging the supercell, tightening accuracy and displacing along the mode.","st":"tested"},{"h":"DFPT","p":"Linear response at any q directly, also giving effective charges, dielectric tensors and electron–phonon coupling.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Machine-learned potentials","p":"Universal potentials handle phonons for most materials but with systematic biases; reliability for anharmonic properties is still being tested.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>κ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Force constants by central differences."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext>supercell</mtext></mrow></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>κ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Interpolating supercell force constants to any q."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>κ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>κ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The non-analytic correction (Gaussian units): Born effective charges Z* and the high-frequency dielectric tensor."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>FD</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>central differences</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two errors of finite displacements: anharmonic truncation and force noise."}],"example":{"q":"Using no symmetry at all, how many supercell calculations does a central-difference force-constant calculation need for a 5-atom primitive cell (SrTiO₃, say) in a 3 × 3 × 3 supercell? How many atoms does the supercell hold? And with cubic symmetry?","steps":["The supercell holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>135</mn></mrow></semantics></math> atoms. Only the 5 atoms of one primitive cell need moving (the supercell’s translations give the rest), each in 3 directions with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></semantics></math> calculations.","In cubic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mn>3</mn><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> there are only 3 inequivalent atoms (Sr, Ti and three equivalent O). One direction each suffices for Sr and Ti, and O needs two (along the Ti–O bond and perpendicular to it); at sites with inversion symmetry the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math> step can also be saved. Altogether only a handful of calculations, and phonopy finds this minimal set automatically."],"a":"30 calculations of 135 atoms without symmetry, and just a few with it. Bear in mind that cubic SrTiO₃ genuinely has imaginary frequencies at low temperature (the antiferrodistortive soft mode), which must be judged real or spurious as described above."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-baroni1987\" data-ref=\"baroni1987\">Baroni, Giannozzi &amp; Testa 1987</a> · <a href=\"#ref-giannozzi1991\" data-ref=\"giannozzi1991\">Giannozzi et al. 1991</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gonze1997\" data-ref=\"gonze1997\">Gonze &amp; Lee 1997</a> · <a href=\"#ref-parlinski1997\" data-ref=\"parlinski1997\">Parlinski, Li &amp; Kawazoe 1997</a> · <a href=\"#ref-baroni2001\" data-ref=\"baroni2001\">Baroni et al. 2001</a> · <a href=\"#ref-togo2015\" data-ref=\"togo2015\">Togo &amp; Tanaka 2015</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batatia2022\" data-ref=\"batatia2022\">Batatia et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-deng2023\" data-ref=\"deng2023\">Deng et al. 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-togo2023\" data-ref=\"togo2023\">Togo 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-pota2024\" data-ref=\"pota2024\">Póta et al. 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-loew2025\" data-ref=\"loew2025\">Loew et al. 2025</a>"}}};
